<section class="shell legal sh-guide" aria-labelledby="sh-guide-title">
  <h2 id="sh-guide-title">How to use this shift handover template</h2>
  <p>A shift handover, also called a shift handoff or, in many plants and control rooms, a shift turnover, is the short exchange in which one shift passes the state of the work to the next. This page is a structured template for it: fill in the five parts of the form, and the sheet below it becomes the handover the next shift reads.</p>

  <h3>Choose a template and its numbers</h3>
  <p>Pick the type of work: warehouse, hotel front office or delivery station. Each template comes with six numbers to report at handover and a six-point handover check. You can rename any number or add your own, so another kind of operation can start from the closest template and still report its own figures.</p>

  <h3>Write down open issues, each with one owner</h3>
  <p>Use one line per issue: what is still open and what the risk is, a priority, one person on the next shift who owns it, and a deadline. Tick an issue off once it is solved. When you close the shift, every issue still open moves to the next handover, marked with the number of times it has been carried over.</p>

  <h3>Go through the handover check together</h3>
  <p>Hand over face to face, with the sheet in hand: it is the agenda for a short conversation, not a substitute for one. Work through the six points of the check; the person taking over then confirms the takeover, once they have read the handover and asked their questions.</p>

  <h3>Print it, save it as a PDF or send it on</h3>
  <p>A finished handover can be printed, saved as a PDF or copied as text. To pass it to another device, send it as a link, a QR code or a file, encrypted with a transfer code; give the code separately, in person or by phone, never in the same message as the link. Names of people are left out unless you choose to include them.</p>
  <p>The handover is saved in this browser. It leaves the device only when you print, copy, save or send it, or as an encrypted copy if your team uses team sync. Whatever you share by email or a messaging app passes through that service, so check it for guest or personal details first.</p>

  <h3>A short example</h3>
  <p>An invented late shift in a warehouse, outbound, docks 1–6:</p>
  <ul class="tips">
    <li><strong>Numbers:</strong> 4,180 units handled; 320 units of backlog at handover, counted and sorted; damage 0.4%.</li>
    <li><strong>Open issue, high priority:</strong> the door of dock 4 does not close fully. Owner: the night shift lead, by 23:00. Carried over once.</li>
    <li><strong>Heads-up:</strong> two trailers for the first early-shift wave are booked for 05:30.</li>
    <li><strong>Safety:</strong> one near miss with a pallet truck at dock 2, recorded.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>To see a complete handover in the form, use “Load an example shift” above.</p>
